**W3-D1-Django_Setup**

---

**What and Why:**
Django is a Python web framework — it gives you everything needed to build a backend API fast. Instead of writing raw Python server code from scratch, Django handles routing, database connections, authentication, admin panel — you just build on top of it. Today you install Django, create your first project, understand every file and folder, and run the dev server. This is the foundation — every week from now builds on top of what you set up today.

---

```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 1: INSTALL DJANGO AND CREATE PROJECT
# ============================================================

# STEP 1 — Make sure venv is activated first
# (venv) must show in terminal before anything else
venv\Scripts\activate   # Windows
source venv/bin/activate # Mac/Linux

# STEP 2 — Install Django
pip install django

# Confirm installed:
django-admin --version
# shows version like 5.0.1


# STEP 3 — Create Django project
django-admin startproject myproject .
# django-admin = Django's command line tool
# startproject = command to create new project
# myproject = name of your project
# . (dot at end) = create project IN current folder
# WITHOUT dot: creates extra nested folder — messy
# WITH dot: cleaner structure — always use the dot


# STEP 4 — See what was created
# Your folder now looks like:
#
# backend-dev/
# ├── venv/
# ├── manage.py          ← Django's command tool for YOUR project
# └── myproject/         ← project configuration folder
#     ├── __init__.py    ← makes this a Python package
#     ├── settings.py    ← ALL project configuration lives here
#     ├── urls.py        ← main URL routing — connects URLs to views
#     ├── wsgi.py        ← used when deploying to production (Render)
#     └── asgi.py        ← used for async features (ignore for now)
```

---

```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 2: UNDERSTAND EVERY FILE — NO CONFUSION LATER
# ============================================================

# manage.py
# Django's command tool for YOUR project
# you'll use this EVERY DAY:
# python manage.py runserver     ← start dev server
# python manage.py migrate       ← apply database changes
# python manage.py createsuperuser ← create admin user
# python manage.py startapp      ← create new app
# NEVER edit manage.py manually


# myproject/settings.py — MOST IMPORTANT FILE
# controls everything about your project
# open this file and find these sections:

# SECRET_KEY = long random string — never share this publicly
# DEBUG = True during development, False in production
# ALLOWED_HOSTS = [] — empty during dev, add domain in production

# INSTALLED_APPS = [           ← list of active apps in project
#     'django.contrib.admin',  ← admin panel
#     'django.contrib.auth',   ← user authentication
#     'django.contrib.contenttypes',
#     'django.contrib.sessions',
#     'django.contrib.messages',
#     'django.contrib.staticfiles',
# ]
# when you create your own apps — you add them here

# DATABASES = {                ← database configuration
#     'default': {
#         'ENGINE': 'django.db.backends.sqlite3',  ← SQLite by default
#         'NAME': BASE_DIR / 'db.sqlite3',         ← database file
#     }
# }
# Week 3 Saturday — you change this to PostgreSQL


# myproject/urls.py — MAIN URL ROUTING
# connects incoming URLs to the right view
# think of it as a traffic director
# all requests come here first — then forwarded to app's urls.py


# myproject/wsgi.py
# Web Server Gateway Interface
# used when deploying to Render/production server
# don't touch this file now


# myproject/asgi.py
# Async version of wsgi
# used for WebSockets, real-time features
# ignore completely for now
```

