```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 3: RUN THE SERVER
# ============================================================

# STEP 5 — Run Django development server
python manage.py runserver
# starts server on http://127.0.0.1:8000/
# 127.0.0.1 = localhost = your own computer
# 8000 = port number

# You'll see output like:
# Watching for file changes with StatReloader
# Performing system checks...
# System check identified no issues (0 silenced).
# You have 18 unapplied migration(s). ← ignore this warning for now
# Django version 5.0.1, using settings 'myproject.settings'
# Starting development server at http://127.0.0.1:8000/
# Quit the server with CTRL-BREAK.

# Open browser → http://127.0.0.1:8000/
# You should see Django's default welcome page
# "The install worked successfully! Congratulations!"
# Green rocket image = Django is running correctly

# CTRL+C = stop the server
```

---

```bash
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 4: FIRST MIGRATION — IMPORTANT STEP
# ============================================================

# Stop server first (CTRL+C)
# Run this command:
python manage.py migrate
# Django comes with built-in tables (users, sessions, admin)
# migrate = creates these tables in SQLite database
# you'll see output like:
# Applying contenttypes.0001_initial... OK
# Applying auth.0001_initial... OK
# Applying admin.0001_initial... OK
# ...

# After migrate — that "18 unapplied migrations" warning disappears
# db.sqlite3 file is created in your folder
# this is your database file (SQLite — temporary, replace with PostgreSQL Saturday)

# Run server again:
python manage.py runserver
# no more migration warnings
```

